Now settings toggle & handler. Check `refreshPanel` call for settings. Add handler line. Also toggle label "Character voices". Maybe add "Preview" button? Let's decide. The user didn't ask for settings; adding a toggle is good. But how can the user hear different character voices? They show during story. There is no way to cycle. Fine.

But since speech synthesis may not have multiple voices on Linux, our pitch profiles still differ. On Android Chrome, voices might be limited too. On iOS, many voices. Good.

Let's add the settings row after Sound. Also if unsupported disabled.

Handler:
```js
else if (act === 'settingsVoice') {
  const s = g.state.settings;
  s.voice = s.voice === false;
  if (!s.voice) this.audio?.stopSpeaking();
  else this.audio?.primeSpeech();
  g.save(); this.refreshPanel();
}
```
Default semantics: `s.voice === false` flips false→true, true→false. If undefined, `=== false` gives false so assigns false? Wait `s.voice === false` is false when undefined, so `s.voice = false`, toggling off. Since newState always sets true and load merges defaults, undefined shouldn't happen. But older in-memory state initialized from newState before our change? At runtime after reload, settings come from newState with voice true. Fine. But to be safe: `s.voice = s.voice === false ? true : false;`. Same. If undefined, turn on? Better: `const next = s.voice === false ? true : false;`. That treats undefined as on and toggles off. Hmm, actually UI on-state uses `s.settings.voice !== false`, so undefined shows on. Toggle should turn it off. Fine.

Settings HTML uses `s.settings.voice !== false ? 'on' : ''`.

Also maybe voice row disabled when unsupported. We'll conditionally render toggle or chip. I need to ensure `this.audio` exists (constructor param). Yes.

Potential issue: `.toggle` is button, `disabled` maybe not styled, use chip.

Add after sound row.

